  "knowledgePattern": "Express the rectangle width and limiting upper edge in one height parameter",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Choose one size parameter",
      "body": "<p>Use the height of the bottom edge to locate its two contacts with the modulus graph. Express the width using that height.</p>",
      "recap": "Splitting a modulus at its centre gives the two straight boundary formulas.",
      "pitfall": "The rectangle is not centred beneath a horizontal upper boundary.",
      "pause": "Find the two contact coordinates and their separation.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-function-mapping",
          "reminder": "Splitting a modulus at its centre gives the two straight boundary formulas."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the tighter upper boundary",
      "body": "<p>Compare the upper-line heights at the two vertical sides. The whole top edge must remain below the line.</p>",
      "recap": "Rectangle area is width times height, with both lengths derived from the actual enclosing geometry.",
      "pitfall": "The higher upper-line value cannot determine the rectangle top if the other side would lie outside.",
      "pause": "Write the area in one variable and state its feasible interval.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b2-l02",
          "reminder": "Rectangle area is width times height, with both lengths derived from the actual enclosing geometry."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimise within the feasible interval",
      "body": "<p>Differentiate the area expression. Use its derivative sign and the feasible endpoints to identify the maximum.</p>",
      "recap": "A derivative changing from positive to negative establishes an interior maximum.",
      "pitfall": "Check zero-width and zero-height boundaries as well as the interior candidate.",
      "pause": "Evaluate and attain the maximum area.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l19",
          "reminder": "A derivative changing from positive to negative establishes an interior maximum."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Let the bottom edge have height h. Its two contacts with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> have x-coordinates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so the width is h.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The upper boundary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> increases with x. Therefore the rectangle top can rise only to its value at the left side, namely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The rectangle height is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and feasibility requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>′</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. This is positive for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and negative for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. The interior stationary point therefore gives the maximum; the two feasible endpoints give zero area. Substitution gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, answer F.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> As a check, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> gives the same attainable maximum.</p>"
}
